\f1\fs22 Invited reception guests milled around the expansive top-floor terraces that adjoined the Gravity Well restaurant, sipping 18th-century champagne that had been recently discovered in a previously unknown network of catacombs beneath a Benedictine abbey outside \'c9pernay. Inside the restaurant, staff were efficiently setting up more tables to cater for the unexpected attendance of The High Elvis and his extensive entourage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi mingled with the Airborne on the terraces. Alexander was indignant; Eloise was amused; Sarah was ambivalent, although she hoped the presence of the Elvi might mean that Tony might also attend.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Conversation between Airborne and Elvi was stilted and awkward, mostly initiated by Airborne guests determined to prove their ability to act cultured in any situation, and thereby demonstrate their indefatigable superiority.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I don\'92t meet very many of your kind,\'94 one Airborne matriarch said to a pair of Clambake series Elvi, \'93but I hope it doesn\'92t offend if I say, you all look the same to me. Doesn\'92t it get confusing?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No ma\'92am,\'94 one said, with trademark Elvi charm. \'93I know exactly who I am.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And so do I,\'94 said the other.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah and Alexander circled among the guests, Alexander hovering around the clumps of the highest cliques of Airborne, never noticing they afforded him the barest minimum of polite recognition before closing ranks with cold shoulders, and striking up conversations regarding matters in which he had no competency. Alexander didn\'92t mind, as long as the cameras kept rolling and his empty glasses were quickly replaced. His stomach rumbled with anticipation of the evening\'92s meal. Eloise had agreed to the most exclusive option the restaurant offered\'97the renowned Warren Buffet Buffet, which was famous for incorporating the highest quality ingredients, and the best value stocks for all the soups and sauces.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was a common misconception that the Gravity Well was the most expensive restaurant in Ulyssia; at the time, that accolade belonged to Futures, a restaurant so expensive, it issued its own financial derivative instruments which a diner could trade to speculate on the price fluctuations of dishes, wines, antacids, executive toilet paper, and even individual ingredients, thereby hopefully minimising the magnitude of the final bill. Most Airborne usually did rather poorly, having accrued their fortunes not by their own acumen, but by inheritance or legislated guarantees of passive income. But they were Airborne, so who gave a tax?\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>There was a rumour that a financial analyst, one Eustice MacArbitrage (personal pronouns: my and mine)\'97who was skilled in currency fixing, data mythology and machine ignorance\'97had found a combination of derivative futures instruments that guaranteed a pay-out every time mine dined there. The management of Futures emphatically discredited such claims as urban myth. Yet all forms of dessert-for-entree swaptions were swiftly discontinued, and no trace of Ms MacArbitrage\'92s body was ever found.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A waiter rang a triangle and called everyone to table. Alexander and Sarah were ushered into an anteroom where they were offered lounges to rest on while the guests were seated. A camera crew fussed about and documented every minute detail. Sarah was glad Eloise had put her foot down and quashed Alexander\'92s whim for a live broadcast. It seemed Eloise could say no to her petulant son after all.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When all was ready, Alexander and Sarah made their triumphant entry, and promenaded to the main table where Eloise, Hayley and Kathy awaited. Sarah made demure melancholic smiles for the applauding guests, and cold stares for the cameras. She tried not to fixate on the empty chair where Tony should be sitting. From the back of the room, the Elvi rose as one and sang, 
\f2\i Glory, glory Holy Momma
\f1\i0 . Sarah\'92s heart leapt as she caught a glimpse of Tony, the Great Teacher, standing alongside The High Elvis.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Glory, glory Holy Momma
\f1\i0 \

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>of the One After the One!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the front of the space where the windows met the far wall, a bandstand and dance area had been improvised. Sarah shot a questioning glance at Eloise. \'93I thought you\'92d booked a string quartet,\'94 Sarah said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise shrugged. \'93The Elvi insisted they play, so I ordered the quartet to go busk in the streets.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93On the ground?\'94 Sarah was incredulous. \'93Under the Layer?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m paying them danger money, covering their health insurance for life, and assigning a guard detail. The Elvi will be more fun, don\'92t you think?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah reflected on the relationship between Eloise and the Elvi with suspicion, and wondered what its true foundation was.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Haloren Smythe, dressed as a waiter, kept his head down and attended tables out of sight or away from Alexander and Sarah, and from Chanta. His earlier thorough investigation regarding the guest list and his on-site reconnaissance determined they were the only three who might recognise him. His eyes throbbed, unaccustomed to his empathy tech being switched off\'97he couldn\'92t risk it attracting attention and knew it would serve him no good when the time came. As he bussed plates between kitchen and table, and attended to the whims of haughtier guests, he frequently slid his hand inside his jacket and, for peace of mind, fondled his Point of Order.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Between courses, various bands of Elvi came up to the stage and performed low-key songs of love and tenderness, of longing and rejection. The songs spoke to Sarah; her vision blurred and her gaze drifted towards the table where The High Elvis and the Great Teacher sat playing drinking games. The sight of Tony gave her a great sense of calm. She didn\'92t realise she was smiling until Alexander leaned close and said into her ear, \'93It\'92s good to see you smile, Sarah.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah was startled. \'93Oh, you frightened me.\'94 She laid her hands protectively on her stomach and wrenched up a smile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander was warmed. \'93That\'92s better.\'94 He waved a camera to come closer to catch the scene more intimately. \'93I have noticed you don\'92t look happy. Sarah, I am perfectly aware of how being married\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Joint-ventured,\'94 Sarah corrected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander swallowed. \'93I know being with such a celebrity as me may bring certain trials. But I am here to protect you from all this.\'94 He waved the camera closer. \'93I want you to know, I am truly honoured that you would allow me to breathe the same air as you, let alone marry you.\'94 Alexander paused for Sarah to correct him, but she only fluttered her eyelids and sucked in a little breath, so he continued. \'93You must know how beautiful you are.\'94 His eyes fell to the bread roll on his plate, while monitoring the camera from the corner of his eye. He fought off tears.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Alex, we have exchanged cheques.\'94 Sarah touched his face and decided to fake it for the rest of the evening. \'93I haven\'92t been feeling well lately, a little nauseous that\'92s all.\'94 Which was true.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nauseous? Are you okay?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I am fine. It\'92s just\'85\'94 She pat her tummy and hoped he\'92d understand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander blinked then smiled, 
\f2\i Ahah!
\f1\i0  then frowned. \'93Sarah, is there something wrong with the food?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As they were finishing the fifth course, Kathy leaned over the table as if she would share a secret. \'93I\'92ve heard a rumour The High Elvis himself will perform tonight.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh?\'94 Hayley added, exaggerating her interest.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92ll be incognito, though.\'94 Kathy sat back in her seat and somehow made herself seem more diminutive alongside Hayley. \'93He\'92ll be disguised as an Elvis.\'94 Hayley laughed, and shared a glance with Kathy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the plates were being cleaned away, a quintet of Elvi, all named Chad Gates, took the stage.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93This is a song called Hawaiian Wedding,\'94 said the front Chad, who the Airborne could not tell apart from any of the other Chads.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Almost as soon as the music started, Sarah felt a sudden calm and well-being. She was overcome with warmth as if Little Clinton himself was comforting her. In her belly, he kicked and giggled. Above the Gravity Well\'92s dome, pink and green auroras swirled and danced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After each course, a band of Elvi took the stage and performed. When a troupe of Elvi sang 
\f2\i Evolve Me Tender
\f1\i0 , the whole room fell silent. Even the most cynical of patrician Airborne nobility were touched by these performing sideshow grifters, and communed with the extraordinary experience of Elvi channelling the well of universal Talent.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Little Clinton was playing soccer with Sarah\'92s bladder. Or it could have been snooker. She would have excused herself for the conveniences, but a squad of waiters arrived and laid plates of the next course. A pair of palm-sized crustaceans, firm-shelled and ruddy, curled on a cloud-shaped pillow of something pureed. They stared up at Sarah like a pair of curious furless mice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise demonstrated the simplicity of prising the meat from their shells, and Sarah felt it would be disrespectful to take leave to relieve herself. Little Clinton was resting for half-time anyway.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis rose, joined the next band of performers, and unwrapped a microphone from its stand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Little Clinton resumed the second half of his 
\f2\i in utero
\f1\i0  soccer game with renewed vigour, and almost kicked Sarah off her seat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Please excuse me,\'94 she said to the table.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis counted the band in. As the music swelled, some in the room swooned. The tune would be recognised in any passably functioning universe as The Wonder of You.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander\'92s ears pricked up. \'93I know this one,\'94 he said to camera and winked at Hayley.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As The High Elvis began the first verse, Alexander became consternated. \'93Those are not the right words,\'94 he declared to Eloise, then repeated it to camera. Possessed of a heroic need to save the day, he launched from the table, strode to the bandstand, seized the microphone from The High Elvis, and presumed to sing the mondegreens he took to be correct.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Accounts of what happened next are many and conflicting. Some say it took The High Elvis sixteen bars to recover from the shock of being upstaged; other accounts say four bars. Some say The High Elvis threw the first punch, but that has been widely discredited\'97The High Elvis commanded a guard detail who would gladly throw punches on his behalf. Some accounts claim Eloise never secured a refund for the courses not served, and the restaurant succeeded in suing the arms dealer for damages, but that\'92s plain stupid; Eloise always got what she wanted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Whichever way you look at it, Alexander gave the Elvi an excuse to indulge in their favourite sport: punching up in a bar.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo seized upon the opportunity. He couldn\'92t have asked for a better distraction; chairs, tables, flatware, glasses, bottles, cutlery, and flying boiled seafood tracked parabolas through the air and landed with varying degrees of concussion. The circumstances couldn\'92t have suited him better than if he was invisible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What\'92s more, Sarah was alone in the toilet. For now.
